{"warnings":{"main":{"*":"Unrecognized parameter: x."}},"batchcomplete":"","continue":{"gsroffset":4,"continue":"gsroffset||"},"query":{"pages":{"93696581":{"pageid":93696581,"ns":6,"title":"File:Colonization of potato tubers by bacteria.png","index":1,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":1425652,"width":1200,"height":1652,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Colonization_of_potato_tubers_by_bacteria.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":1280,"thumbheight":1762,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Colonization_of_potato_tubers_by_bacteria.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1280,"height":1762},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Colonization_of_potato_tubers_by_bacteria.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colonization_of_potato_tubers_by_bacteria.png","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=93696581","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2020-08-31 04:41:41","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"Colonization of potato tubers by bacteria","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Bacteria|Potatoes","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"Possible routes of colonization of potato tubers by bacteria.<br>\n<p>Microbiota colonizing the rhizosphere, entering the roots and colonizing the next tuber generation via the stolons, are visualized with a red color. Bacteria present in the mother tuber, passing through the stolons and migrating into the plant as well as into the next generation of tubers are shown in blue.\n</p>\n<ul><li>The soil is the main reservoir for bacteria that colonize potato tubers</li>\n<li>Bacteria are recruited from the soil more or less independent of the potato variety</li>\n<li>Bacteria might colonize the tubers predominantly from the inside of plants via the stolon</li>\n<li>The bacterial microbiota of potato tubers consists of bacteria transmitted from one tuber generation to the next and bacteria recruited from the soil colonize potato plants via the roots</li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2019-11-08","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0223691\">[1]</a>\n</p>\n<bdi><span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_object_identifier\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Digital object identifier\">doi</a></span>:<a href=\"//doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0223691\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0223691\">10.1371/journal.pone.0223691</a></bdi>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"Franziska Buchholz, Livio Antonielli, Tanja Kosti\u0107, Angela Sessitsch and Birgit Mitter","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-sa-4.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}}}]},"139423426":{"pageid":139423426,"ns":6,"title":"File:Inoculation of potato tubers (cv. \u201cRusset Norkotah\u201d) with Phytophthora erythroseptica zoospores fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg","index":4,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":626057,"width":3110,"height":2484,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Inoculation_of_potato_tubers_%28cv._%E2%80%9CRusset_Norkotah%E2%80%9D%29_with_Phytophthora_erythroseptica_zoospores_fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg/1280px-Inoculation_of_potato_tubers_%28cv._%E2%80%9CRusset_Norkotah%E2%80%9D%29_with_Phytophthora_erythroseptica_zoospores_fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1280,"thumbheight":1022,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Inoculation_of_potato_tubers_%28cv._%E2%80%9CRusset_Norkotah%E2%80%9D%29_with_Phytophthora_erythroseptica_zoospores_fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Inoculation_of_potato_tubers_%28cv._%E2%80%9CRusset_Norkotah%E2%80%9D%29_with_Phytophthora_erythroseptica_zoospores_fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg/1280px-Inoculation_of_potato_tubers_%28cv._%E2%80%9CRusset_Norkotah%E2%80%9D%29_with_Phytophthora_erythroseptica_zoospores_fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1280,"height":1022,"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Inoculation_of_potato_tubers_%28cv._%E2%80%9CRusset_Norkotah%E2%80%9D%29_with_Phytophthora_erythroseptica_zoospores_fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Inoculation_of_potato_tubers_%28cv._%E2%80%9CRusset_Norkotah%E2%80%9D%29_with_Phytophthora_erythroseptica_zoospores_fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inoculation_of_potato_tubers_(cv._%E2%80%9CRusset_Norkotah%E2%80%9D)_with_Phytophthora_erythroseptica_zoospores_fmicb-10-00131-g003.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=139423426","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2023-10-23 19:50:40","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"Inoculation of potato tubers (cv. \u201cRusset Norkotah\u201d) with Phytophthora erythroseptica zoospores fmicb-10-00131-g003","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Phytophthora|Vaccinations|Diseases and disorders of Solanum tuberosum|Zoospores|Diseases and disorders of plants due to Phytophthora","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"Figure 3. Inoculation of potato tubers (cv. \u201cRusset Norkotah\u201d) with Phytophthora erythroseptica zoospores. Either tuber slices (upper panels) or whole tubers (lower panels) of potato were inoculated with 5 zoospores suspended in 10 \u03bcl sterile distilled water (A1,A2), 5 zoospores suspended in 10 \u03bcl exudate derived from high-density zoospore suspension (1 \u00d7 104 spores/ml) (B1,B2), and 100 zoospores suspended in 10 \u03bcl sterile distilled water (C1,C2). Arrows indicate inoculation position.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"05 February 2019","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmicb.2019.00131/full\">https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmicb.2019.00131/full</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"He Jiang, Hye Weon Hwang, Tongling Ge, Barbara Cole, Brian Perkins, Jianjun Hao","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 4.0","source":"comm